      "problem": "Given that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
